00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:35.240
This past week brought concrete advances in making large models faster and more efficient on actual hardware.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.300
Agent systems moved from single tools toward layered setups where one model oversees another.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:48.480
Multimodal designs also stepped forward with native handling of synchronized audio, video, and text streams.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:55.040
OpenAI expanded its coding agent reach while security-focused initiatives gained new partners.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:02.220
Two separate training techniques showed how to cut compute time without changing model architecture or losing performance.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:15.440
Sikana AI and NVIDIA introduced 12, a technique that applies L1 regularization to induce over 99% sparsity in the feed-forward layers of large language models.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:24.380
They then translate that sparsity into actual throughput gains using new sparse data formats and fused kuda kernels.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:34.580
The reported speed-ups are 20.5% for inference and 21.9% for training, while downstream performance remains essentially unchanged.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:46.380
Most current sparsity works stays theoretical, yet 12 demonstrates concrete GPU-level improvements that local developers can test through updated X-Lama V3 tools.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:02:00.180
News research released token superposition training, a two-phase pre-training technique that averages contiguous token embeddings into bags during the first phase, before reverting to standard next token prediction.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:12.820
The approach works on dense models from 270 million to 3 billion parameters, and a 10 billion active 1 billion MOE without any architecture, tokenizer, or optimizer changes.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:19.880
It delivers up to 2.5 times wall clock speed-ups at matched flops while preserving downstream performance.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:27.700
Teams doing continued pre-training or scratch training on mid-size models can now cut training time dramatically on existing hardware.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:39.040
Thinking Machines Lab released a research preview of TML, interaction, small, a native multimodal architecture built for continuous human AI collaboration.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:53.040
The 276 billion parameter MOE model activates 12 billion parameters and ingest synchronized 200 millisecond chunks of audio, video, and text, through a multi-stream time-align design.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:00.920
A real-time interaction component maintains full duplex exchange while an asynchronous background model handles sustained reasoning.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:12.420
Revision cuts visual token usage by approximately 46% for computer use agents while lifting success rates three points on OS world and web-tail bench.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:25.100
Open AI launched Daybreak, a cyber security program that integrates frontier AI models with codec security, its coding-focused agentic system, and a network of security partners.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:36.240
The effort targets developers, enterprise teams, researchers, and government defenders who need to detect, validate, and patch software vulnerabilities earlier in the development process.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:43.200
Daybreak automates detection, validation, and response while respecting existing security rules and workflows.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:50.420
Open AI began rolling out codecs as a native preview on iOS and Android in all supported regions.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:56.580
The mobile version supports full agent workflows with phone to Windows desktop connection arriving shortly.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:04.680
This moves codecs from desktop bound tool to always available coding partner, matching the reach of consumer chat apps.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.740
Fin announced, Fin operator, an AI agent purpose built to manage the company's customer facing Fin agent.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:25.780
Operator acts as data analyst, knowledge manager, and debugger by generating charts from conversation metrics, ingesting product PDFs to update help articles, and tracing failed conversations to root causes in guidance rules.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:35.440
It runs on cloud rather than Fin's own Apex models because the tasks resemble software engineering work more than customer service resolution.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.280
Every change requires explicit human approval before going live.

